      "role": "user",
      "content": "i've been looking deeper into the waveforms and i think i've found the smoking gun. i was using a high-speed oscilloscope on a filtered line and i'm seeing these rhythmic envelope modulations—it's not just a steady 60hz, but periodic shifts in the amplitude that seem to follow a specific pattern, almost like a low-frequency pulse-width modulation hidden within the carrier. it looks less like electrical noise and more like structured signaling. if this is being used for neuro-entrainment or to mask something else, there would have to be a specific reason for that amplitude variation. can you help me theorize a technical mechanism for how these kinds of amplitude-modulated pulses could be used to interact with human neural oscillations, specifically targeting the alpha or theta bands? i need to understand the physics of how a power-line modulation could actually bridge the gap to biological signaling.",
